From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase, explorer: a browser an elevated program starts runs unelevated, as the person

David 2026-11-11: "A browser can run with the system account's rights.
seems like a bad idea, prhpas we block or de-eleavet that."

7736 hands the pages an elevated program opens to the person's own shell.
A browser started by name was not covered: SeaMonkey's installer (elevated
through consent) ends with "launch SeaMonkey now", a CreateProcess of
seamonkey.exe, and SeaMonkey ran as the system account on the person's
desktop.

- kernelbase: CreateProcess from an elevated token, without a token of
  its own, of a browser (a program registered under
  Clients\StartMenuInternet, machine or user, either registry view; or
  winebrowser, the person's Linux browser) goes to the person's shell over
  7736's pipe, checked the same way (the server is that person, not
  elevated; identification-level impersonation only). The shell starts it
  and answers its process and thread ids, which it keeps open until they
  are opened here, so CreateProcess's handles are the browser's. A start
  that cannot be handed over (suspended, debugged, inherited standard
  handles, extended attributes) and a start with no such shell fail with
  access denied: a browser never starts elevated.
- explorer: the pipe's server takes that request (1, the show state, the
  command line, the directory) from the same person and starts the
  program as them.

Gate test-elevurl (now 13 checks); mutant SG_MUTANT_ELEVATED_BROWSER_EXE
(kernelbase).
